# "Much" with things you can count. From ESL and common-error lists; examples are ours.
# why: Much goes with things you can't count (much time, much water); many goes with things you can count (many people, many mistakes).
expr counted [people, friends, things, times, students, users, customers, employees, items, mistakes, errors, questions, problems, issues, options, ideas, books, cars, members, participants, children, visitors, guests, reasons, changes, tasks, files, emails, years, days, hours, places, countries]
expr main <(much @counted), much>

let message "Use `many` with things you can count."
let description "Fixes `much people` → `many people`."
let kind "Agreement"
let becomes "many"
let strategy "MatchCase"

test "There are much people interested in the course." "There are many people interested in the course."
test "We found too much mistakes in the draft." "We found too many mistakes in the draft."
test "How much students signed up?" "How many students signed up?"

allows "There is much interest in the course."
allows "We had too much time."
allows "How many students signed up?"
